        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Water' drawing (start)] The screen displays a whiteboard. In the center, the word \"Water\" is written in black. An arrow points from the word to a drawing of a cup in the bottom right corner. A notification at the top of the screen reads \"Jayden has joined\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 303 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: Drawing of a cup and the word 'Water' (start)] The whiteboard shows the word \"Water\" handwritten in black. An arrow points from the word to a simple drawing of a cup.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 310 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Water' drawing (start)] The whiteboard shows the word \"Water\" written in black, with an arrow pointing from it to a simple drawing of a cup. The student, Jayden, is in the session.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 266 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears on the screen: \"Ms. Parker has joined\". The whiteboard is cleared, and the view changes to a grid showing the student's and tutor's icons.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 70 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 11 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The student's video feed disappears from the screen. The tutor remains in the session alone.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The empty panel where the student's video was is removed, and the tutor's video panel expands to fill the space. A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Ms. Parker has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 23 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi Jayden.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 8 seconds]\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: How are you doing today?\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Good.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: Did you have a good weekend?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The tutor and student's video feeds appear on screen.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Okay, that's good.\nTurn 8. TUTOR: Let's see.\nTurn 9. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 28 seconds]\nTurn 9. TUTOR: Okay Jayden, can you give me a highlight of your past week? And then something that something low that happened in your last week.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 7 seconds]\nTurn 10. STUDENT: Uh, well, I really don't know.\nTurn 11. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 11. TUTOR: Or maybe let's just try two things that made you happy recently.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 74 seconds]\nTurn 12. TUTOR: It could be something simple like, oh, like I played video games or, oh, my mom got me In-N-Out or something like that.\nTurn 13. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 13. STUDENT: Um.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 6 seconds]\nTurn 14. STUDENT: Well, I mean, I just stayed home.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 15. TUTOR: Okay. I think that's also a happy thing. So I'll take that.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: Um. Okay, let me pull up the... the...\nTurn 17. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 51 seconds] STUDENT nods.\nTurn 17. TUTOR: Okay, we're still working on angles, right?\nTurn 18. TUTOR: Or that's something that you still want to work on? Okay.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 21 seconds]\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen goes black.\nThe screen shows a list of math topics. The tutor's cursor clicks the \"Start topic\" button next to \"Angles on parallel lines 1\".\nTurn 19. TUTOR: Okay. Do you want to do angles part two, angles on parallel lines, or basic angle facts four?\nTurn 20. STUDENT: About angles two.\nTurn 21. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A loading screen for the an online learning platform platform appears, displaying \"Angles - Part 2\".\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Which of the following is true?' (start)] A problem appears on the screen titled \"Angles - Part 2\". It shows a straight horizontal line with a ray extending upwards and to the left from a point on the line. This creates two angles around the point on the line. The larger, reflex angle is labeled 'p'. The smaller, acute angle is labeled 'q'. The question is \"Which of the following is true?\". The options are: A) p + q = 90°, B) p + q = 100°, C) p + q = 180°, D) p + q = 360°.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: Okay. So which of the following is true? And then you can let me know which one you think it is.\nTurn 23. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 48 seconds]\nTurn 23. STUDENT: Um, C.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds] Tutor clicks on option C, then clicks the \"Check\" button.\nThe screen displays a message \"C is correct\".\nStudent's cursor points to the reflex angle 'p', then to the straight line itself.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: Okay. Let me put C.\nTurn 25. TUTOR: Yeah. Very good. Can you explain that?\nTurn 26. STUDENT: So, uh, now you would think it would be more